    "millsfield:images_texts": [
      "First Flight Over the North Pole\nMay 12, 1926 • Spitzbergen, Norway\n090\nNORGE\nNI\n114001)\nGRENADA\n30c\nNORGE\n200th Anniversary of Manned Balloon Flight 1783-1983\n8000N\n917\nNORGE\n12.05.84\n200\n12.05.84",
      "International Tributes to the History of Flight\nFirst Flight Over the North Pole\nOn May 11, 1926, the airship Norge, designed and\nPole first, aviation experts contest the claim.\nbuilt by Umberto Nobile of Italy, stood ready at Spitz-\nThe full-color cachet shows the two crafts involved\nbergen, Norway. If all went well, it would carry Nor-\nwegian explorer Roald Amundsen and American\nin the race to the Pole. On the ground is Richard\nfinancier Lincoln Ellsworth on an historic journey\nByrd's Fokker trimotor, \"Josephine Ford.\" Aloft is\nover the North Pole.\nAmundsen and Ellsworth's airship, the Norge.\nMint Stamp: The mint stamp on this Cover was issued\nThirteen hours after lift-off — at 1:00 a.m. on May\n12 - thed oship sleed over the North Pole. If then\nby Grenada in 1983 to commemorate the bicentennial\nof manned balloon flight. It pictures the Norge dur-\ning its flight from Spitzbergen, Norway to Teller, Alaska\nThe flight had unexpectedly become something of\na race. Two days earlier, Americans Richard Byrd and\nFloyd Bennett set out for the Pole in the \"Josephine\nFord.\" Despite Byrd's assertion that he reached the\nvia the North Pole.\nPostmark: This Cover was postmarked in Spitzber-\ngen, Norway on May 12, 1984, the anniversary of the\nfirst flight over the North Pole.\n© Postal Commemorative Society\nPrinted in U.S.A.\n2019.056.027"
